Python practical — Build a Linear Regression model


import pandas as pd
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression

# Historical training data
data = {
    "monthly_income": [20000, 30000, 40000, 50000, 60000],
    "monthly_spending": [12000, 18000, 24000, 30000, 36000]
}

df = pd.DataFrame(data)

# X = input features; y = target
X = df[["monthly_income"]]
y = df["monthly_spending"]

# Create and train the model
model = LinearRegression()
model.fit(X, y)

# Predict spending for a new customer
new_customer = pd.DataFrame({
    "monthly_income": [70000]
})

prediction = model.predict(new_customer)

print("Slope:", model.coef_[0])
print("Intercept:", model.intercept_)
print("Predicted spending:", prediction[0])



Step 3: Understand the code
Code	                             Meaning
pd.DataFrame(data)	               Creates a table from the data
X = df[["monthly_income"]]	       Selects the input feature as a two-dimensional table
y = df["monthly_spending"]       	 Selects the target values
LinearRegression()	               Creates the regression model
model.fit(X, y)                    Learns the coefficient and intercept
model.coef_	                       Returns the learned slope
model.intercept_                  Returns the learned intercept
model.predict(...)                Predicts a numerical value

