Q1. What is Linear Regression?
Linear Regression is a supervised learning algorithm used to predict continuous numerical values by modeling a linear relationship between input features and a target variable.
Q2. Explain the equation \(y=mx+b\).
Here, \(y\) is the predicted value, \(x\) is the input feature, \(m\) is the slope, and \(b\) is the intercept.
Q3. What is the cost or loss function in Linear Regression?
A common objective is to minimize Mean Squared Error (MSE), which measures the average of squared differences between actual and predicted values. Ordinary Least Squares minimizes the sum of squared residuals.
Q4. What is the difference between MAE and RMSE?
MAE measures average absolute error. RMSE takes the square root of average squared error, so large errors have a greater influence on it.
Q5. What does model.fit(X, y) do?
It estimates the model parameters from the training data. For Linear Regression, these include the coefficients and intercept.
Q6. What does model.predict(X) do?
It uses the trained model to estimate target values for the supplied input features.
Q7. What assumptions should we consider in Linear Regression?
For standard statistical inference, important assumptions include linearity, independent errors, constant error variance, and normally distributed errors for certain inferential procedures. Multicollinearity is also important in multiple regression. Prediction can still be useful when some assumptions are imperfect, but interpretation and reliability may be affected.
Q8. What is the difference between Linear Regression and Logistic Regression?
Linear Regression predicts numerical values. Logistic Regression is commonly used for classification and estimates class probabilities, such as the probability that a transaction is fraudulent.

